  "generated_text": "First, we perform the $45^\\circ$ rotation around the origin. A $45^\\circ$ rotation of a complex number $a + bi$ is equivalent to multiplying by $e^{i\\pi/4} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} + i\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$.\n\n\\[\n(-3 - 8i) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} + i\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right) = \\frac{-3}{\\sqrt{2}} - \\frac{8i}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{-3 - 4i}{\\sqrt{2}}\n\\]\n\nNext, we perform the dilation centered at the origin with a scale factor of $\\sqrt{2}$. This dilation scales the complex number by $\\sqrt{2}$.\n\n\\[\n\\frac{-3 - 4i}{\\sqrt{2}} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} = \\frac{-3\\sqrt{2} - 4i\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} = -3 - 4i\n\\]\n\nThus, the resulting complex number after both transformations is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-3 - 4i}\n\\]",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[-1*4*i - 3, '-3 - 4i']",
    "gold_answer": "A $45^\\circ$ rotation in the counter-clockwise direction corresponds to multiplication by $\\operatorname{cis} 45^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} + \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}},$ and the dilation corresponds to multiplication by $\\sqrt{2}.$  Therefore, both transformations correspond to multiplication by $\\left( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} + \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right) \\sqrt{2} = 1 + i.$\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(0.5 cm);\n\npair A = (-3,-8), B = (5,-11);\n\ndraw((-4,0)--(6,0));\ndraw((0,-12)--(0,2));\ndraw((0,0)--A,dashed);\ndraw((0,0)--B,dashed);\n\ndot(\"$-3 - 8i$\", A, SW);\ndot(\"$5 - 11i$\", B, SE);\n[/asy]\n\nThis means the image of $-3 - 8i$ is $(-3 - 8i)(1 + i) = \\boxed{5 - 11i}.$",
    "is_correct": false
  },
